# ---------------------------------------- CL1 INFRASTRUCTURE AND IaC
LZ-01|Cloud|squad:infra|Sprint 0|task|P0|5|Choose the open-source infrastructure baseline and record it in an ADR|OpenTofu as the IaC tool. Decide the compute substrate, the Kubernetes distribution and the environment isolation model, and say why. Decide before you provision anything.
LZ-02|Cloud|squad:infra|Sprint 0|task|P0|5|OpenTofu remote state on MinIO with state locking|Shared, versioned, locked state. Never on a laptop, never in the repo.
LZ-03|Cloud|squad:infra|Sprint 1|task|P0|8|Build the network module: private networks, firewall rules and a WireGuard entry point|Reusable across dev, staging and production with no copy-paste. No node exposed directly to the internet.
LZ-04|Cloud|squad:infra|Sprint 1|task|P0|5|Provision every compute node as code, with no manual console steps|If you cannot destroy and rebuild the environment from an empty state, this ticket is not done.
LZ-05|Cloud|squad:infra|Sprint 2|task|P1|5|Reusable OpenTofu modules for PostgreSQL, Valkey and MinIO|Versioned modules with inputs, outputs and examples so product squads self-serve without asking you.
LZ-06|Cloud|squad:infra|Sprint 2|task|P1|3|Labelling convention plus drift detection in CI|Every resource tagged with owner, environment and squad. A nightly plan that fails loudly when reality has drifted from code.
LZ-07|Cloud|squad:infra|Sprint 3|task|P1|5|Secrets with OpenBao and the External Secrets Operator|Rotation, short-lived credentials, and a secret scanner in CI so nothing sensitive reaches the repo.
LZ-08|Cloud|squad:infra|Sprint 4|task|P2|5|Policy as code with OPA and Conftest on every OpenTofu plan|Block public object storage and wide-open firewall rules at plan time. Prevent the mistake rather than detect it later.
LZ-09|Cloud|squad:infra|Sprint 3|task|P0|5|Automated backups for PostgreSQL and MinIO with retention and encryption|Scheduled, encrypted, stored away from the thing they are backing up, with a documented retention period. Backups that live on the same node are not backups.
LZ-10|Cloud|squad:infra|Sprint 4|task|P0|5|Prove it — restore the staging database from backup and time the recovery|A backup nobody has restored is not a backup. Destroy, restore, record the actual elapsed time, and write what went wrong the first time.
# -------------------------------------------- CL2 CONTAINER PLATFORM
PL-01|Cloud|squad:platform|Sprint 0|task|P0|5|Provision the Kubernetes cluster with OpenTofu|k3s or kubeadm, your call, recorded in an ADR. No cluster created by hand.
PL-02|Cloud|squad:platform|Sprint 1|task|P0|5|Ingress with Traefik, plus cert-manager and Let's Encrypt|Every service reachable over HTTPS with certificates that renew themselves.
PL-03|Cloud|squad:platform|Sprint 1|task|P0|5|Namespace per squad with resource quotas and Cilium network policies|One squad's runaway pod must not starve another squad. Default-deny between namespaces.
PL-04|Cloud|squad:platform|Sprint 2|task|P1|5|A Helm chart library any software squad can adopt in under an hour|Sensible defaults for probes, limits, config and secrets. Squads set their own values — you provide the shape.
PL-05|Cloud|squad:platform|Sprint 2|task|P1|5|Horizontal pod autoscaling and pod disruption budgets, proven with k6|A load test that visibly scales the deployment up and back down.
PL-06|Cloud|squad:platform|Sprint 3|task|P1|8|Workload identity and mTLS between the six services|SPIFFE/SPIRE or Linkerd. No shared static secrets between services.
PL-07|Cloud|squad:platform|Sprint 3|spike|P2|3|Decide whether a service mesh is justified at this scale|Complexity has a cost. Timebox two days and write an ADR either way.
PL-08|Cloud|squad:platform|Sprint 4|task|P1|3|Cluster upgrade runbook, rehearsed in staging|Someone who was not in the room must be able to follow it.
PL-09|Cloud|squad:platform|Sprint 4|task|P1|8|Cluster disaster recovery — rebuild staging from code and backups|Delete staging deliberately and bring it back. Document the real RTO and RPO you achieved, not the ones you hoped for.
# -------------------------------- CL3 CI/CD AND DEVELOPER EXPERIENCE
CD-01|Cloud|squad:cicd|Sprint 0|task|P0|5|Reusable GitHub Actions workflow: lint, test, Trivy scan, SBOM, build, cosign sign|One workflow all six software squads call, rather than six divergent copies.
CD-02|Cloud|squad:cicd|Sprint 0|task|P0|3|Harbor registry with image signing, vulnerability scanning and retention|Signed images only. Old images cleaned up so storage does not creep.
CD-03|Cloud|squad:cicd|Sprint 1|task|P0|5|GitOps with Argo CD using an app-of-apps pattern|Dev auto-syncs from the repo. The cluster's desired state lives in git, not in someone's shell history.
CD-04|Cloud|squad:cicd|Sprint 2|task|P0|5|Promotion from dev to staging to production with an approval gate|The same signed artefact is promoted, never rebuilt per environment.
CD-05|Cloud|squad:cicd|Sprint 2|task|P1|5|Ephemeral preview environment for every pull request|A namespace or vcluster per PR, torn down automatically when it closes.
CD-06|Cloud|squad:cicd|Sprint 3|task|P1|5|A database migration step that is safe to run inside a pipeline|Forward-only, reversible, and safe to run twice. Atlas or Flyway.
CD-07|Cloud|squad:cicd|Sprint 3|task|P1|5|Progressive delivery with automatic rollback|Argo Rollouts with a canary step that aborts on a bad health signal, then notifies.
CD-08|Cloud|squad:cicd|Sprint 4|task|P2|5|Developer portal or onboarding script: laptop to first deploy in under an hour|Backstage, or a well-written script. Test it on someone who has never seen the repo, and time them.
# ----------------------------------- CL4 OBSERVABILITY, SRE AND COST
OB-01|Cloud|squad:sre|Sprint 0|task|P0|3|Centralised logs with Loki and Alloy|Structured logs, sensible retention, and no secrets or personal data in log lines.
OB-02|Cloud|squad:sre|Sprint 1|task|P0|5|Prometheus and Grafana, with a starter dashboard per service|Give each squad a working RED dashboard as a starting point. They extend it themselves in ticket D4.
OB-03|Cloud|squad:sre|Sprint 1|task|P0|5|Distributed tracing with the OpenTelemetry Collector and Tempo|One trace should follow a single customer request across every service it touches.
OB-04|Cloud|squad:sre|Sprint 2|task|P0|5|SLOs and error budgets with Sloth or Pyrra, agreed with each squad|Sit with each squad. An SLO written alone by the platform team is not an SLO.
OB-05|Cloud|squad:sre|Sprint 2|task|P1|5|Alertmanager, an on-call rotation simulation, and runbook links|Every alert links to a runbook or it gets deleted. Include the software squads in the rotation.
OB-06|Cloud|squad:sre|Sprint 3|task|P1|5|Cost visibility with OpenCost, broken down by squad and namespace|Publish it every Wednesday. Visible cost changes behaviour faster than a lecture.
OB-07|Cloud|squad:sre|Sprint 3|task|P1|3|Budget alerts and automatic shutdown of idle dev environments|Nights and weekends off. Report the saving at demo day.
OB-08|Cloud|squad:sre|Sprint 4|task|P1|5|Run a game day: inject failure with Litmus and measure detect and recover time|Break something without warning while the software squads are on call. Write an honest, blameless incident review afterwards.
OB-09|Cloud|squad:sre|Sprint 4|task|P1|3|Add a data-loss scenario to the game day|Do not only break availability. Break data, and see whether anyone can get it back. Coordinate with CL1 on LZ-10 first.
# ----------------------------------------------------- CROSS-CUTTING
XX-01|Cross-cutting|squad:cross-cutting|Sprint 0|chore|P0|2|Agree branch protection, pull request rules and CODEOWNERS as a cohort|Decide the rules together so they are owned rather than imposed.
XX-02|Cross-cutting|squad:cross-cutting|Sprint 0|task|P0|3|Publish the API contract style guide and a shared error format|Six services with six different error shapes is exactly what this prevents.
XX-03|Cross-cutting|squad:cross-cutting|Sprint 0|task|P1|2|Write the synthetic data generator shared by every service|One source of fake customers, sites and orders so the demo hangs together.
XX-04|Cross-cutting|squad:cross-cutting|Sprint 0|task|P0|2|Write the escalation ladder onto the wall and agree to follow it|Thirty minutes alone, then docs, then your squad, then another squad, then a facilitator. See docs/COMPETENCY-FRAMEWORK.md.
XX-05|Cross-cutting|squad:cross-cutting|Sprint 1|task|P1|3|Set up the ADR directory and record the first four decisions|Template plus the decisions already made in Sprint 0.
XX-06|Cross-cutting|squad:cross-cutting|Sprint 3|task|P0|5|End-to-end integration test covering order to invoice to payment|Crosses four squads. Whoever picks it up has to coordinate, which is the lesson.
XX-07|Cross-cutting|squad:cross-cutting|Sprint 4|task|P0|5|Accessibility audit of the portal against WCAG 2.2 AA|Keyboard navigation, contrast, screen reader labels. Raise findings as tickets.
XX-08|Cross-cutting|squad:cross-cutting|Sprint 4|task|P0|5|Threat model the portal and log the findings as tickets|A STRIDE walkthrough with all ten squads represented.
XX-11|Cross-cutting|squad:cross-cutting|Sprint 4|task|P1|3|Write the service acceptance checklist — nothing is live until it passes|Runbook exists, SLO published, dashboard live, backups proven, on-call named, rollback tested. Every service is then assessed against it.
XX-12|Cross-cutting|squad:cross-cutting|Sprint 5|task|P1|3|Decommission the preview and dev environments and prove nothing was orphaned|Tearing things down cleanly is part of the job. Show the cost drop and the empty state.
XX-09|Cross-cutting|squad:cross-cutting|Sprint 5|task|P0|3|Prepare the demo day showcase script and runbook|Who says what, in what order, and a fallback if the live demo fails.
XX-10|Cross-cutting|squad:cross-cutting|Sprint 5|task|P0|3|Write your placement portfolio: README, architecture note and three STAR stories|Pull the stories straight from your own tickets. This is what you take to an interview.
